#: ../../source/algorithms/operator_splitting.rst:271
msgid "Summary"
msgstr "小结"

#: ../../source/algorithms/operator_splitting.rst:273
msgid "Splitting methods belong to the family of *decomposition algorithms*: they break a large problem into smaller subproblems handled separately (here, by the clients and the server), so that each subproblem is easier to solve and easier to parallelize. In theory, they bring many federated algorithms under one umbrella and -- unlike ``FedAvg``/``FedProx`` -- their limits are guaranteed to be correct (:ref:`FedSplit <fl_alg_os_fedsplit>`) or to approach a stationary point of the true objective (:ref:`FedDR <fl_alg_os_feddr>`). The price is bookkeeping: besides the model parameters, every node (server and clients) maintains auxiliary variables (the reflected blocks :math:`z`, the blended copies :math:`\\omega`), which adds memory and computation overhead."
msgstr "算子分裂法是* 分解算法* (Decomposition Algorithms) 的一大类：把大规模问题拆成若干分别处理的小问题（在这里，由子节点与中心节点分别承担），使每个子问题更易求解、更易并行。在理论上，它把众多联邦算法纳入统一框架——并且与 ``FedAvg``/``FedProx`` 不同，其极限保证是正确的（:ref:`FedSplit <fl_alg_os_fedsplit>` ），或逼近真实目标的稳定点（:ref:`FedDR <fl_alg_os_feddr>` ）。代价是簿记：除模型参数外，每个节点（中心与子节点）还要维护辅助变量（反射块 :math:`z` 、混合拷贝 :math:`\\omega` ），带来额外的存储与计算负担。"

